From: Octessera <octessera@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: bcm2835-mmc: handle the controller in a threaded interrupt

On the Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W the onboard Wi-Fi SDIO controller (mmcnr) can
hang mid DMA transfer ("Controller never released inhibit bit(s)"), which
stalls the shared DMA engine and the I2S audio DMA with it. Running the
controller in PIO avoids the hang, but PIO copies the data inside the hard
interrupt handler on whichever CPU takes the interrupt, including the
real-time audio core.

Handle the controller in a threaded interrupt instead. The line stays masked
until the thread finishes (IRQF_ONESHOT), the thread takes the host lock with
interrupts disabled as before, and userspace can pin the irq thread away from
latency-critical CPUs.

---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/bcm2835-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/bcm2835-mmc.c
@@ -989,8 +989,9 @@
 	struct bcm2835_host *host = dev_id;
 	u32 intmask, mask, unexpected = 0;
 	int max_loops = 16;
+	unsigned long flags;
 
-	spin_lock(&host->lock);
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&host->lock, flags);
 
 	intmask = bcm2835_mmc_readl(host, SDHCI_INT_STATUS);
 
@@ -1037,7 +1038,7 @@
 		intmask = bcm2835_mmc_readl(host, SDHCI_INT_STATUS);
 	} while (intmask && --max_loops);
 out:
-	spin_unlock(&host->lock);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&host->lock, flags);
 
 	if (unexpected) {
 		pr_err("%s: Unexpected interrupt 0x%08x.\n",
@@ -1370,7 +1371,12 @@
 	init_waitqueue_head(&host->buf_ready_int);
 
 	bcm2835_mmc_init(host, 0);
-	ret = request_irq(host->irq, bcm2835_mmc_irq, IRQF_SHARED,
+	/*
+	 * Handle the controller in a threaded interrupt so PIO transfers run in
+	 * a schedulable kernel thread that can be kept off latency-critical CPUs.
+	 */
+	ret = request_threaded_irq(host->irq, NULL, bcm2835_mmc_irq,
+				   IRQF_SHARED | IRQF_ONESHOT,
 				   mmc_hostname(mmc), host);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(dev, "Failed to request IRQ %d: %d\n", host->irq, ret);
